---
/**
 * unsubscribed.astro — Newsletter unsubscribe confirmation page.
 *
 * SINGLE RESPONSIBILITY
 *   Render a static, human-facing "you've been unsubscribed" acknowledgement.
 *   This is the *landing/thank-you* page a reader sees AFTER the unsubscribe has
 *   already been processed. It performs NO logic itself: no token parsing, no
 *   database write, no auth check. It is pure confirmation copy.
 *
 * WHERE IT FITS IN THE ARCHITECTURE
 *   The actual unsubscribe mutation (flipping a subscriber's status / deleting
 *   their row in Supabase) happens elsewhere — typically a Cloudflare Pages
 *   Function (an /api/... endpoint) that validates a signed, single-use
 *   unsubscribe token embedded in the "unsubscribe" link of a Resend email.
 *   That Function does the security-sensitive work (signature/token check +
 *   RLS-guarded Postgres write) and then redirects the browser here on success.
 *   Because this page is served statically by Cloudflare Pages, it must be
 *   treated as PUBLIC and UNAUTHENTICATED: anyone can open /unsubscribed
 *   directly. That is fine precisely because the page reveals nothing and does
 *   nothing — see the security note below.
 *
 * IMPORTS / DEPENDENCIES
 *   - BaseLayout.astro — the site-wide HTML shell (<head>, meta/OG tags, header,
 *     footer, global styles). This page only supplies a title, description, and
 *     body slot; all chrome comes from BaseLayout.
 *   No other runtime deps: no Supabase client, no Turnstile, no Dodo, no fetch.
 *
 * WHAT DEPENDS ON THIS PAGE
 *   - The unsubscribe Pages Function / email-link flow, which redirects here as
 *     its success destination. Its route ("/unsubscribed") is therefore a
 *     de-facto contract: renaming or moving this file would break that redirect
 *     target and leave unsubscribing readers on a 404. Keep the path stable.
 *
 * SECURITY / PRIVACY ASSUMPTIONS (why this page is deliberately "dumb")
 *   - No PII on the page: it never echoes the email address, subscriber id, or
 *     token back into the DOM. So the URL is safe to share/log/crawl and there
 *     is nothing to leak even though the page is world-readable.
 *   - Not idempotent-sensitive: reloading or opening /unsubscribed directly has
 *     zero side effects because the state change lives in the upstream Function,
 *     not here. This avoids the classic pitfall of doing the unsubscribe *in*
 *     the confirmation page (which link-prefetchers/scanners would auto-trigger).
 *   - GOTCHA for the returning developer: do NOT be tempted to move the actual
 *     unsubscribe write into this Astro page. It is statically pre-rendered and
 *     has no request context, no token validation, and no server trust boundary.
 *     The token check + RLS-protected mutation must stay in the Pages Function.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

{/*
  Static confirmation markup only. `title` / `description` feed BaseLayout's
  <head> (browser tab + OG/meta). The em-dash em-dash-suffixed title matches the
  site's page-title convention. `description` is intentionally generic so the
  page carries no subscriber-identifying text even if indexed or previewed.
*/}
<BaseLayout title="Unsubscribed — areyoustillreading" description="You've been unsubscribed.">
  <h1>You've been unsubscribed</h1>
  {/* Reassurance + a self-service path back: resubscribe lives on the homepage
      (the newsletter signup form), so we don't duplicate that form here. */}
  <p>You won't receive any more emails. You can resubscribe anytime from the homepage.</p>
  {/* &larr; is the HTML entity for the left-arrow glyph; kept as an entity so it
      renders reliably regardless of source encoding. */}
  <p><a href="/">&larr; Home</a></p>
</BaseLayout>
